            {
                "entry_id": "c9c8910f-4807-32ac-86e7-f7a6b68edab1",
                "word": "picaresque",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "4800acb8-3b8c-4b44-a21d-08cafb9733f2",
                        "entry_id": "c9c8910f-4807-32ac-86e7-f7a6b68edab1",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "picaresques"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "58252586-ec52-3525-b0a2-ee308ede49ca",
                        "entry_id": "c9c8910f-4807-32ac-86e7-f7a6b68edab1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A picaresque novel."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "67b6adda-20a5-4c9f-917b-7052cb9af50c",
                                "sense_id": "58252586-ec52-3525-b0a2-ee308ede49ca",
                                "text": "Du sublime au ridicule il n'y a qu'un pas [From the sublime to the ridiculous there is only one step]; and that step in Spain was taken by Diego Hurtado de Mendoza (already dealt with in his higher walk), the originator of the Picaresque, or low rogue's march novels, of which his Lazarillo de Tormes was the type— [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "71e488a7-c0fa-4a5b-be08-03e51f34e69d",
                                "sense_id": "58252586-ec52-3525-b0a2-ee308ede49ca",
                                "text": "[T]he word picaro, which is almost synonymous with rogue, [...] has given a name to a whole series of novels in Spain called \"Picaresques,\" the heroes of which are adventurers."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ef08b07d-32d5-4a8e-8f7f-f7ab28146296",
                                "sense_id": "58252586-ec52-3525-b0a2-ee308ede49ca",
                                "text": "And we had agreed completely as to the main stream in the history of the novel … as to its passing from Lope da Vega and the Spanish picaresques, by way of [Danie] Defoe and Richardson, to [Denis] Diderot, Stendahl and [Gustave] Flaubert [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5cf39bed-bbff-4762-93f1-488df2b01ca3",
                                "sense_id": "58252586-ec52-3525-b0a2-ee308ede49ca",
                                "text": "In picaresques, the protagonist usually acts as narrator to provide coherence and unity to the genre's inherent structural weakness—its episodic plot."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
